WEBVTT

NOTE
Journey: Advocates - Week 26 Leader Video
Unit 7, Lesson 2: Falsehood

1
00:00:05.970 --> 00:00:11.450
So 7.2, we call it falsehood. And this lesson really is a

2
00:00:11.450 --> 00:00:17.010
response to a common atheist objection that if God was so

3
00:00:17.010 --> 00:00:22.870
big and mighty and powerful and sovereign and all good, then he would have

4
00:00:22.870 --> 00:00:27.030
prevented evil. He wouldn't have allowed it in the first place.

5
00:00:27.130 --> 00:00:31.410
I mean, think about it. If God can do these things, if God can just speak and

6
00:00:31.410 --> 00:00:35.990
suddenly we have a universe, then why can't he prevent evil.

7
00:00:36.330 --> 00:00:41.970
Years ago, I did a debate with an atheist, and this was sort of his argument.

8
00:00:42.090 --> 00:00:47.970
His argument was very complex, but let me sort of summarize it as quickly as I can.

9
00:00:48.390 --> 00:00:54.090
He basically said that if God existed, God would have created

10
00:00:54.090 --> 00:00:59.890
a world in which only God-like objects existed and he called that

11
00:00:59.890 --> 00:01:05.790
God world. And if this God world were an actual world, then all the pieces of

12
00:01:05.790 --> 00:01:08.150
God world would be just like God.

13
00:01:08.490 --> 00:01:11.470
There would be no suffering, there would be no evil, there would be no nothing.

14
00:01:11.570 --> 00:01:16.530
Everything would be absolutely perfect on equal playing field to God.

15
00:01:17.090 --> 00:01:19.150
And my response was pretty simple.

16
00:01:19.310 --> 00:01:23.250
It took me some time to figure out exactly what he was saying and then hit me.

17
00:01:24.270 --> 00:01:29.030
I said, well, one, why do you think this is the case?

18
00:01:29.130 --> 00:01:31.090
And he said, well, because there's evil and suffering in the world.

19
00:01:32.050 --> 00:01:33.870
So, well, let's put that aside for a second.

20
00:01:34.290 --> 00:01:36.930
Is there anything that God cannot do?

21
00:01:37.730 --> 00:01:41.470
And he said, well, if God is all powerful, then there's nothing that God cannot do.

22
00:01:41.830 --> 00:01:46.870
That's why he, if he can't prevent evil, then God must not exist.

23
00:01:47.590 --> 00:01:53.350
I said, that's not the case. Just because God is all-powerful and sovereign

24
00:01:53.350 --> 00:01:58.390
and omniscient and all-awesome, then

25
00:01:58.390 --> 00:02:02.190
does that mean that there's nothing that God cannot do?

26
00:02:02.590 --> 00:02:06.490
So unfortunately, the old song, my God is so great, my God is so big, there's

27
00:02:06.490 --> 00:02:12.170
nothing, that's actually not necessarily true because God could not create

28
00:02:12.170 --> 00:02:17.270
a world in which only God-like objects or God-like things, things that are

29
00:02:17.270 --> 00:02:22.930
equivalent to him existed because if that were the case then

30
00:02:22.930 --> 00:02:27.630
God would not exist because he wouldn't be God anymore.

31
00:02:28.390 --> 00:02:32.770
He would just be equal to everything else then everything would be the same as God

32
00:02:32.770 --> 00:02:37.190
and it would be all things would be God and that would take us into a form of

33
00:02:37.190 --> 00:02:38.430
pantheism.

34
00:02:39.470 --> 00:02:45.290
So his argument didn't work but it's sort of this understanding that well why

35
00:02:45.290 --> 00:02:48.790
wouldn't God do that? Well, because simply put, there's some things that God

36
00:02:48.790 --> 00:02:53.650
actually cannot do. And what's important about this lesson is to help students

37
00:02:53.650 --> 00:02:57.390
understand that God is not the one that's responsible for evil.

38
00:02:57.430 --> 00:03:01.050
God did not create it. It's like I said last week, it's not a thing to be created

39
00:03:01.050 --> 00:03:03.770
because evil is not a thing. God did not create it.

40
00:03:03.810 --> 00:03:09.430
God is not responsible. And although God is doing something to remedy the situation,

41
00:03:09.810 --> 00:03:14.450
he still has to allow us to have free will to make our own choices.

42
00:03:14.910 --> 00:03:19.530
So this week, I would get your students thinking about the character and nature of

43
00:03:19.530 --> 00:03:23.770
who God is. Have them think about, just like in the intro activity, we asked them

44
00:03:23.770 --> 00:03:27.590
about things that it's impossible for humanity to do, but what about things that are

45
00:03:27.590 --> 00:03:31.850
impossible for God to do? What are some other things that maybe God cannot do?

46
00:03:32.070 --> 00:03:37.490
Have your students sort of think that through, of what makes God, God, and what

47
00:03:37.490 --> 00:03:40.650
maybe limits does God actually have.

48
00:03:41.290 --> 00:03:45.930
Now, I don't mean fun things like can God create a rock so big he can't lift it and

49
00:03:45.930 --> 00:03:47.030
those strange questions like that.

50
00:03:47.050 --> 00:03:48.190
That's not what I'm talking about.

51
00:03:48.710 --> 00:03:52.030
But things like God cannot contradict himself.

52
00:03:52.470 --> 00:03:58.410
God cannot lie. So have your students think that through to help paint a better

53
00:03:58.410 --> 00:04:04.130
picture of the nature of God and exactly what he's doing in this world to

54
00:04:04.130 --> 00:04:08.050
remedy the evil and the suffering that is so prevalent.

55
00:04:08.470 --> 00:04:14.350
So God has morally justified reasons for allowing the temporary existence of

56
00:04:14.350 --> 00:04:16.450
evil and suffering in our world.

57
00:04:16.730 --> 00:04:19.490
That may seem like a cop-out, like what?

58
00:04:20.710 --> 00:04:25.650
That's the best you've got? But remember what Scripture says and what Paul says in 1

59
00:04:25.650 --> 00:04:29.730
Corinthians is that we only see things in part right now.

60
00:04:29.810 --> 00:04:33.970
We don't have a full picture. There's still quite a bit of mystery going on.

61
00:04:34.630 --> 00:04:39.850
The example I always used to give my students was the example of being a kid or

62
00:04:39.850 --> 00:04:44.490
parenting my own kids is that I may know what's best for my kids, they may not

63
00:04:44.490 --> 00:04:48.630
realize that I know what's best and what I'm telling them they have to do or what

64
00:04:48.630 --> 00:04:52.930
I'm putting them through may seem like a terrible, terrible thing for them in that

65
00:04:52.930 --> 00:04:57.690
moment. The whole world is coming crashing down on them yet I know exactly what I'm

66
00:04:57.690 --> 00:05:01.970
doing and it's for their good. And that may be the case sometimes, and sometimes

67
00:05:01.970 --> 00:05:06.650
it's just the case that evil is just evil, and evil just happens.

68
00:05:07.090 --> 00:05:13.050
And God doesn't like it any more than we do, but he has to allow it, even

69
00:05:13.050 --> 00:05:18.070
if only for a time. And as we'll see later on, when we get to more of the pastoral

70
00:05:18.070 --> 00:05:23.450
approach to this question of evil, is that it can be used for something

71
00:05:23.450 --> 00:05:29.010
exceptionally good. So in your small groups, I want you to start to brainstorm

72
00:05:29.010 --> 00:05:31.250
a little bit about what good can come from evil.

73
00:05:32.170 --> 00:05:37.250
But I also want you to, again, be very aware when I keep beating this drum, be very

74
00:05:37.250 --> 00:05:38.750
aware of the questions that are coming.

75
00:05:39.030 --> 00:05:42.950
If the questions didn't come last week, they may be coming this week and they may be

76
00:05:42.950 --> 00:05:45.090
coming next week. They may be coming all of the weeks.

77
00:05:46.090 --> 00:05:49.870
And be aware of some of the things that your students are going through.

78
00:05:50.510 --> 00:05:52.890
By now, you probably know your students really well.

79
00:05:52.950 --> 00:05:54.410
You probably know their families.

80
00:05:54.570 --> 00:05:59.730
You probably have talked with their parents and you've connected with their families

81
00:05:59.730 --> 00:06:00.390
on some level.

82
00:06:01.490 --> 00:06:05.470
Do your best to be aware of some of the things that your students are going through

83
00:06:05.470 --> 00:06:10.970
for two reasons. One, so you can pray with them and pastor them along, walk with

84
00:06:10.970 --> 00:06:14.950
them and be there for them in the way that you need to be.

85
00:06:15.030 --> 00:06:19.950
That goes without saying. But the other is when we're dealing with these kinds of

86
00:06:19.950 --> 00:06:24.550
issues, you want to have an awareness and a sensitivity so that you don't say

87
00:06:24.550 --> 00:06:28.550
anything that would touch the wrong nerve with your students.

88
00:06:28.590 --> 00:06:32.610
Sometimes you may want to, a little bit, to get a response out of students and to

89
00:06:32.610 --> 00:06:38.270
move them along, but you also want to be very, very aware of where they are so that

90
00:06:38.270 --> 00:06:43.330
you can minister to them the best possible way without saying them back at all.

91
00:06:43.450 --> 00:06:49.270
So rather than going down the easy road and just simply doubting God because of the

92
00:06:49.270 --> 00:06:55.110
evil that we see around us. We can actually look to him for hope and know that he

93
00:06:55.110 --> 00:06:59.830
has a plan, know that he has a purpose, and know that of all the things that we're

94
00:06:59.830 --> 00:07:02.490
going through, it's not for nothing.

95
00:07:03.050 --> 00:07:06.770
We can look to him for significant hope.

